{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-5569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Out of the ten contestants in an Olympic event, how many possible groups of $3$ medalists are there, regardless of which medal they win?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-5569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ contestants, so $n=10.$ We want to select $3$ of those contestants for the medal positions, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential groups of medalists is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{10}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{10!}{(10-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{7!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 9\\cdot 8}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{720}{6} \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-5569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8566bf353e7fa00567dda68ed01cf77519969e9b838ad1345aa319fdaf3a4eb2"} {"question" "q-5569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-5569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "120"] ["math" "5\\,040"] ["math" "604\\,800"] ["math" "720"] ["math" "920"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-5569" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "120"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given any two points in a plane, there is a unique line that passes through both points. Given six points in a plane, what is the maximum number of unique  lines that can be made using these points?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ points, so $n=6.$ We want to select $2$ of those points for each of the lines, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the maximum number of unique lines is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{4!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84964" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8566bf353e7fa00567dda68ed01cf77519969e9b838ad1345aa319fdaf3a4eb2"} {"question" "q-84964" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84964" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "15"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84964" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $(\\frac{25}{0}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that $(\\frac{25}{0})$ means the same thing as ${}_{25}{C}_{0}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=25$ and $r=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{25}{C}_{0} & =\\frac{25!}{(25-0)!0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{25!}{25!0!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{25!}}{\\cancel{25!}\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8566bf353e7fa00567dda68ed01cf77519969e9b838ad1345aa319fdaf3a4eb2"} {"question" "q-84993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "50"] ["math" "75"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-84993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-86823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "There were $9$ chess players who took part in a chess tournament. How many games were played in the tournament if each player faced every other player exactly once?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-86823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ players, so $n=9.$ We want to select $2$ of those players for each of the matches, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of games is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{7!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot \\cancel{7!}}{\\cancel{7!}\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{2} \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-86823" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8566bf353e7fa00567dda68ed01cf77519969e9b838ad1345aa319fdaf3a4eb2"} {"question" "q-86823" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-86823" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "17"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "64"] ["math" "72"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-86823" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "36"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-122565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Stefany wants to draw a picture using $5$ colors from her $24$-color crayon box. In how many ways can she pick the $5$ colors?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-122565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $24$ colors, so $n=24.$ We want to select $5$ of those colors for Stefany to draw her picture, so we have $r=5.$\n\nTherefore, the number of choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{24}{C}_{5} & =\\frac{24!}{(24-5)!\\,5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{24!}{19!5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot 23\\cdot 22\\cdot 21\\cdot 20\\cdot \\cancel{19!}}{\\cancel{19!}5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot 23\\cdot 22\\cdot 21\\cdot 20}{5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\,100\\,480}{120} \\\\  & =42\\,504.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980755/state.json" "file_sha256" "c23c2c2554a853042fdac244045e05eebad330293ae3abcfd674c506509c2fb2"} {"question" "q-122565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13